51:**Sub-decision: how cash deposits count (D31).** (i) Out of trading receipts until the underwriter moves them (revision 1). (ii) In trading receipts, with the cash inside them reported as its own figure (M18) and indicators on large deposits, cash-heavy months and cash withdrawn then paid back in. (iii) A firm setting that chooses (i) or (ii). *Chose (ii)*, the owner's answer. (i) lost because it understates a cash business such as a café and makes the underwriter add back every ordinary deposit by hand. (iii) lost because M2 would then mean different things in different packs, so a signed memo citing M2 could not be read without knowing the setting; a firm that wants a deposit out moves it with an override and a reason. What (ii) gives up: personal cash paid in by an owner lifts M2, M9 and M10 unless an indicator catches it or a person moves it (see Consequences).
57:**Sub-decision: where the categorisation rules live.** (i) Keep them as code and add more. (ii) Move the rule tables into packaged, validated data files like the policy YAML; the matching passes (reversals, round trips, pairing, profile names) stay in code. *Chose (ii)*: the lists of acquirers, lenders, cash wording and gambling operators are what changes most often, the v1 table can be kept as data for rollback, and a firm's own rules use the same format.
69:    loader and validation -> categoriser (rule tables, profile, firm rules, overrides)
82:- **C1 Rule tables and categoriser** (src/cma/categorise.py; rule tables as data under src/cma/rules/). Built-in tables v1 (today's) and v2 (the new lists). v2 names cash paid in (counter, deposit machine, Post Office and PayPoint wording) as cash_deposit, and names cash-withdrawal wording that only the cash round-trip indicator reads. Precedence: per-line override, then protected built-in patterns (returned DD, director, capital, shareholder, lender, HMRC), then profile names (directors, own accounts), then firm rules, then the rest of the built-in table, then the default (a debit is supplier, a credit is unexplained). Each line records which rule set its category. A pattern that is ambiguous between buckets resolves to one that needs judgement, never to trading receipts; cash wording is not ambiguous, and counting cash as trading is the owner's decision (D31).
167:- **Security.** *Who can call it:* the operating-system user who owns the case root. The workspace also needs a per-launch token and a loopback Host header (R33). *Trust boundary:* imported files and payer text are checked by the loader and escaped on output (R34). *Secrets:* only an optional model key in the environment, never logged. *Personal data:* kept under one case root readable only by its owner, never committed to git (R31), never in prompts (R30); evidence holds only hashes of the profile. *Risk path:* lending data; the human acknowledgement is the named sign-off bound to a snapshot that includes the policy hash and whether the check was shown (R22). The illustrative outcome is not an acknowledgement. *Worst case:* see Security notes, now including cash paid in to look like trading.
193:**New figures.** No id is ever reused. M1-M16 keep their ids and units. M16 is renamed 'Non-trading money in (share)'; M2 keeps the name Total revenue, and its method sentence now names cash paid in. Values move only on lines a v2 rule now recognises (R10).
214:**Indicators.** Each has an id, a kind, the threshold that fired, evidence ids and a one-line reason. Thresholds live in a packaged, validated YAML file that the firm can replace. Amounts in it are pounds with at most two decimals and are compared in pence. While a threshold still has its shipped value, the workspace and the pack call it illustrative, as they do the policy. Kinds: a large single credit against typical trading receipts; a cash deposit at or above the single-deposit limit; a month with cash above a set share of its trading receipts; cash paid in within a window of days after a cash withdrawal, the two amounts within a tolerance; a payment to a gambling operator; a returned direct debit; a run of consecutive overdrawn days; an irregular credit from a named acquirer. Shipped cash values, subject to the owner question: a single deposit of £1,000 or more, cash above 50% of a month's trading receipts, and a round trip within 7 days and 10% of the amount.
218:**Rule tables.** Packaged YAML under src/cma/rules/. v1 is today's table, frozen. v2 adds acquirers, gateways, marketplaces and delivery platforms; lenders and merchant cash advance providers; wording for own pots and savings; cash paid in (counter, deposit machine, Post Office and PayPoint); cash-withdrawal wording for the round-trip indicator only; HMRC repayments; refunds; and explained non-trading credits. Tables are validated on load like the policy, and each has an id and a hash.
286:| An owner pays personal cash in to lift turnover | It counts as trading receipts (D31). A deposit at or above the limit, a cash-heavy month, or cash paid in soon after a similar withdrawal is flagged; the underwriter moves it to director funding with a reason | R14, R17, R55 |
349:- **CI:** evals extended with truth for every bucket and indicator, the cash ones included; P1-P20 run as tests; the wheel check also loads the rule tables, thresholds, field map and packaged policy from an installed wheel.

